The hero section of this website runs a small n-body gravity simulation on a `<canvas>`. Everything looked perfectly fine but, as you guessed, it actually wasn't. 

I was testing a WebGL glass library when I noticed something weird. The page was reporting a document width of **2402px**... on a **1265px** viewport. This meant something was overflowing.

I love debugging my own code. It feels like a maze with your `Ctrl + F` trying to find everywhere you suspect might be the culprit. Except this time, it was way harder, and something most people my not expect.

Turns out I'd been hiding the problem the entire time with `overflow-x: hidden` on `body`.

The culprit ended up being the canvas itself:

```css
.hero__sim {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
}
```

At first glance, that looks like it should just fill the hero.

Apparently not.

`canvas` is a **replaced element**, which means it has intrinsic dimensions taken from its `width` and `height` attributes. If it's absolutely positioned with `left` and `right` set but `width` left as `auto`, the browser happily uses those intrinsic dimensions instead of stretching it.

Meanwhile my resize handler was doing this:

```js
canvas.width  = Math.floor(W * dpr);
canvas.height = Math.floor(H * dpr);
```

That's exactly what you want for the drawing buffer. On a 2× display though, those attributes end up being twice the CSS size.

So my canvas was actually rendering at around **2360px** wide inside a **1180px** hero, quietly sticking out of the page.

The fix was relatively easy after finding out the root cause. Here if you didn't guess it:

```css
.hero__sim {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
}
```

It's one of those lines that's incredibly easy to forget.

The `width` and `height` **attributes** control the drawing buffer.

The `width` and `height` **CSS properties** control how large the element actually appears on the page.

They're completely different things, and with `<canvas>` you almost always need both.

The funny part is that nothing actually looked broken. The particles rendered perfectly. The layout looked normal. There were no console errors. The only reason I even found the bug was because a completely unrelated library expected `scrollWidth` to match the viewport width.

I guess that's how a lot of bugs are found. Not really because you're looking for them as we're trained and thought during class but because you accidentally trip over them while debugging something else.
